      <div class="cav">The number limit</div>
      <h2 id="numbers">Why do big numbers break in JSON?</h2>
      <p>JSON numbers are stored as double-precision floating point, so only integers up to 2 to the power of 53 minus 1, which is 9,007,199,254,740,991, are guaranteed exact. Push past that and digits silently change. RFC 8259 says so directly: integers in the range up to that value are the ones that implementations will "agree exactly on." <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number/MAX_SAFE_INTEGER" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MDN</a> confirms the same limit as Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER. So a 17-digit order number or a social media post ID can lose its last digit the moment it is treated as a JSON number. The Best Answer Hub Data Converter lets you keep long identifiers as strings so they survive intact.</p>
